  "verbalization": "The visible evidence from the dataset suggests that Ethambutol may be associated with Pulmonary Tuberculosis through off-label use in the context of Mycobacterial Infectious Disease. This association is supported by multiple pathways where Ethambutol is used off-label to treat Mycobacterial Infectious Disease, which in turn is linked to Pulmonary Tuberculosis. The off-label use of Ethambutol is connected to other antimicrobial agents such as Ethionamide and Cycloserine, which are also used in the treatment of Mycobacterial Infectious Disease. These pathways indicate a potential role for Ethambutol in the management of Mycobacterial infections, even outside of its approved indications. The evidence is consistent across different pathways, suggesting a broader role in the treatment of Mycobacterial Infectious Disease, which may include Pulmonary Tuberculosis. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as it is based on off-label use and indirect connections through other antimicrobial agents. The mechanisms involved appear to be related to the treatment of Mycobacterial infections, but the exact therapeutic role of Ethambutol in this context remains uncertain.",
